#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Records that this session made this commit.
#
# pre-push's header says git offers no way to tell sessions apart, and that is
# true of git. It is not true of a session, which knows perfectly well what it
# just committed — it simply had nowhere to write it down. So this writes it
# down, and pre-push stops guessing.
#
# The guess it was making is the reason this exists. It marks the newest commit
# "yours?" and the rest as somebody else's, which is right most of the time and
# wrong exactly when it matters: on 2026-08-25 a session pushed and carried four
# commits from another, having read a list where all four sat under a "not
# yours" marker it had already decided to override. The refusal was not the
# problem. PUB4_PUSH_ALL=1 is one phrase, it answers every objection at once,
# and after the fourth or fifth time it is typed without reading.
#
# A ledger changes what the warning can say. "3 of these 4 are not from this
# session, here they are" is a specific claim about specific commits, and a
# specific claim is much harder to wave through than a general one.
#
# Keyed on PUB4_SESSION when the environment sets one, and otherwise on the
# terminal's own session — the point is only to distinguish concurrent agents in
# one checkout, so anything stable per agent and different between them works.
# With neither, this writes nothing and pre-push behaves exactly as before: no
# ledger is not a claim that a commit is foreign.

require "fileutils"

def session_id
  explicit = ENV["PUB4_SESSION"].to_s.strip
  return explicit unless explicit.empty?

  # The parent pid, which is stable for SOME agents and not others — and the
  # difference is worth stating, because this comment used to claim it was
  # stable full stop.
  #
  # Measured after a day in a shared checkout: this session's shells share one
  # parent, so its commits grouped under a single key. A concurrent agent
  # spawned a fresh parent per command, so its commits landed under 61 keys,
  # one each.
  #
  # Harmless, and worth knowing why. The guard only ever asks whether a commit
  # is MINE, so a key that fragments produces no false claim — it produces no
  # claim at all, which is the safe direction. What it cannot do is group
  # another session's work, and nothing asks it to.
  #
  # Set PUB4_SESSION and none of this applies.
  ppid = Process.ppid
  ppid.positive? ? "ppid-#{ppid}" : ""
rescue StandardError
  ""
end

id = session_id
exit 0 if id.empty?

git_dir = IO.popen(%w[git rev-parse --git-dir], &:read).strip
exit 0 if git_dir.empty?

sha = IO.popen(%w[git rev-parse HEAD], &:read).strip
exit 0 if sha.empty?

ledger = File.join(git_dir, "pub4-session-commits")
FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(ledger))
File.open(ledger, "a") { |f| f.puts("#{id} #{sha}") }
exit 0
